# Version 0.4.0 plans

Before beginning make sure the VERSION is up to date, and create a file called `docs/dev/plans/version-<version number>-phased.md` which is a phased plan for implementing the features, then stop after creating it. If that file exists, the that means the version has been started and you're taking over from where another agent left off. If it doesn't then create the file making sure to also create "checkpoints" which are points after a few phases where the full test suite is ran, and must pass before proceeding. You don't need to run the full tests on every phase because it's going to be too slow. For each checkpoint, if it's not going to trample one another, feel free to use subagents, in fact ideally you should.

Additional things to keep in mind:
- Usability takes priority over accessibilty when the two collide. This app is a personal app, primarily used by me, not one being shared with people who have accessibility needs. It's nice for it to be usable, but not at the cost of functionality or performance.
- When documenting features, in the `dev` docs be sure to include mermaid diagrams, and code references to make it clear and simple for people to find what they need to understand.

## Maintenance

- [x] Check all dependencies and do updates (0.4.0 Phase 1 — playwright + @types/node updated; astro, vitest and TypeScript 7 deliberately deferred, see the phase plan)

## Features & fixes

- [x] Swap over all svelte components to regular web components (0.4.0 Phase 2 — `web/js/*.js`, no build step; framework runtime and bundled mermaid both gone from `dist/_astro/`)
- [x] Replace Astro with a golang system that just builds and exports a bundle fully custom, fully static bundle (0.4.0 Phases 3-9 — one Go binary; `src/`, `scripts/` and Astro deleted at Phase 9, Checkpoint D passed)
  - [x] Keep dependencies to a minimum (2 direct: goldmark + chroma, 1 transitive)
    - [x] Don't bother doing steps like minification either (0.4.0 — `public/` and `web/` are copied byte for byte: no minify, no bundle, no content hash)
    - [x] Ideally the UI should be no-build throughout the entire process until after the final bundle is generated if the user wants to, via some sort of post-processing step (0.4.0 Phase 8 — a `postprocess` block, `--postprocess <cmd>` or `FRZNFORGE_POSTPROCESS`; nothing runs by default, and frznforge itself still never minifies, bundles or hashes)
  - [x] Move the ingest to a more concurrency-friendly approach where as the system is ingesting information it can build the pages for that repo, while fetching data for other repos (0.4.0 Phase 7 — landed as parallel rendering at BOTH levels, repos and pages; the fetch/render overlap was measured as worth a few seconds against a 204 s render, the core-level parallelism as worth half of it. Self-site 3.66 s → 1.84 s vs astro's 8.74 s)
  - [x] Re-use as much of what's already here as you can, but be willing to pull things out or change the architecture if it makes sense (0.4.0 — schema v8 unchanged so `data/` keeps working, `web/` and the e2e suite carried over untouched, the Astro/Svelte/Shiki/marked layers replaced. Architecture that DID change, deliberately: the config is JSONC not TypeScript, rendering is parallel at both levels, and the highlight memo was dropped because chroma made it unnecessary)


## For human

This section is for me to keep track of what I need to manually review, feel free to add to this

- [ ] Decide whether the two JSONC splice engines get folded into one. `cmd/frznforge/entries.go` and `internal/wizard/{entries,edit}.go` implement the same thing twice (~1,600 lines). Of 20 shared functions 11 are byte-identical and 8 differ only cosmetically, but the twentieth had diverged into a data-loss bug (the wizard deleted the scaffolded config's commented-out examples). Fixed and tested on both sides; the fork itself is left for you to rule on — 0.4.0 or 0.4.1.
- [ ] Walk the release checklist, including the item the last two releases both tripped on: the release commit is actually **pushed**. Nothing in 0.4.0 has been committed yet.

## Per change

Required guidence to follow per change

- Check off changes as you go
  - Make sure the TODO is kept up to date so that if a usage limit is hit I'm not left in the dark
- if data model is updated
    - There are no migration issues/regressions with the changes, if there are, make sure migration guide is documented in the changelog
    - There are adequete tests for the system to ensure no synchronization issues, and that the system works both from the UI and has unit tests
- Add Changes to the CHANGELOG.md
    - If a version exists and does not have a release date yet, keep adding to it, if there's no versions without a release date, then bump version (e.g. `v0.1.0 -> v0.2.0`) in the version file, and add a new main heading to CHANGELOG.md for it (e.g. `v0.2.0 (unreleased)`) and add the changes under there.
    - Be brief, 2-4 setnences per change, and if you need more, prompt for approval from the user
      - When writing about **new features** do not include small changes as separate items
        - For example if you added a feature to show a checkbox for `Add all` then later I decide (within the same version) the UI should be a toggle switch. The changelog only needs the notice about the new feature, not the UI update since that's covered by the new feature bullet point. Do include change messages for existing features from other versions. 
    - Add all changes under the following categories as sub-headings:
      - Features
      - Bug Fixes
      - Other
        - For things like
          - Documentation improvements
          - Refinements to existing systems that weren't bugs
          - anything else that doesn't fit in the other sections